# kit template — every placeholder in docker-compose.yml and otel-collector.yml.
#
#   YOU DO NOT COPY THIS FILE. `bin/dev` fetches it from the pinned kit ref and
#   writes .env on first run, because a committed copy of it in nine service
#   repositories is nine files that go stale and nobody notices.
#
#   A service's own .env is git-ignored and holds nothing but OVERRIDES. The
#   one thing that is NOT an override — the kit ref this repository runs — lives
#   in `kit.ref`, committed, beside this file's eventual `.env`.
#
# WHY EVERY VALUE HAS A DEFAULT
#   docker-compose.yml substitutes ${KIT_POSTGRES_PORT:-15500}, so a fresh clone
#   runs with no .env at all. These defaults exist so that the file a developer
#   edits, and the file a teammate has in git, cannot disagree silently.
#
# WHY NOTHING SENSITIVE IS HERE
#   These are development credentials for a container bound to the host
#   loopback. The real ones live in each service's deployment secrets, never in a
#   repo. If you find yourself wanting to put a production credential in this
#   file, you want a secrets manager instead.
#
# tests/validate.sh asserts that every ${KIT_*} used by the two templates
# appears below with a non-empty default. A placeholder used but undocumented
# fails the gate.

# ============================================================ THE PIN IS NOT HERE
# There is deliberately no `KIT_STACK_REF=` line in this file.
#
# The ref of kit this repository runs lives in `kit.ref`, one committed line at
# the repository root:
#
#   $ cat kit.ref
#   babdedc55baae47fc50628467e22c47d7ad9308e
#
# WHY IT IS NOT HERE, WHICH IS THE INTERESTING PART
#   This file becomes `.env`, and `.env` is git-ignored. A pin kept in `.env`
#   exists on exactly one machine: the laptop of whoever ran `bin/dev pin` last,
#   and on no CI runner and no teammate. "One command, always current" then means
#   "one command, whatever this checkout last fetched", and the thing the pin is
#   FOR — that the stack a developer runs is a reviewable, identical statement
#   everywhere — is the thing a `.env` pin quietly destroys.
#
#   `kit.ref` is committed. So the bump is a line in `git diff`, it is reviewed
#   like any other change to the dev loop, and `tests/stack_adoption.py` reads
#   it to decide whether this repository runs a pin at all.
#
# TO MOVE IT, DELIBERATELY:
#   bin/dev pin v0.4.0              # prints the stack diff FIRST, then writes kit.ref
#   bin/dev pin <40-char sha>
#
# For one run without changing the pin:  KIT_STACK_REF=<sha> bin/dev

# Where to fetch it from. Overridable so a self-hoster can mirror kit, and so
# `tests/fetch_test.sh` and `tests/stack_live_test.sh` can point the real code
# path at a local file:// remote rather than a mock of it.
KIT_STACK_URL=https://github.com/cafaye/kit.git

# Set to 1 for a fully local loop with no network. bin/dev then uses only what is
# already on this machine — KIT_STACK_DIR, the cache, or a vendored copy at
# .kit/stack that RECORDS its ref — and fails loudly, naming each of them, when
# none of them holds the pinned ref. It never falls back to an unversioned
# directory: "the stack came up" is a claim, and a claim about WHICH BYTES is the
# whole point.
#
#   # vendor once, on a machine that has the network:
#   git clone --depth 1 "$KIT_STACK_URL" .kit/stack
#   git -C .kit/stack checkout "$(cat kit.ref)"
#   git -C .kit/stack rev-parse HEAD > .kit/stack/.kit-stack-ref
KIT_STACK_OFFLINE=0

# WHERE A VENDORED OFFLINE COPY LIVES. It must DECLARE its ref in
# `.kit-stack-ref` — a directory that merely contains templates/compose/ is not a
# kit checkout at a known version, and accepting one silently is how an offline
# loop stops matching what the team runs.
KIT_VENDOR_DIR=.kit/stack

# WHERE THE STACK'S COMPOSE FILE AND ITS SIBLINGS LIVE. `bin/dev` sets this to
# the fetched tree's templates/compose; you should not set it by hand. It is
# here because tests/validate.sh requires every ${KIT_*} the templates
# interpolate to be documented, and the default `.` is the directory holding
# docker-compose.yml — which is what a hand-copied stack has always meant.
KIT_COMPOSE_DIR=.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- project
# Compose project name. This is the prefix on every container, volume, and
# network, so two checkouts on one machine do not collide. A service sets this to
# its own name — `KIT_STACK_NAME=muse` — which is one line in its .env and the
# reason two services can both be running on one laptop.
KIT_STACK_NAME=cafaye

# ============================================================== THE PORT BLOCK
# kit claims 15000-15999 for the whole stack, one hundred per service:
#
#   15000  grafana            the UI, on http://localhost:15000
#   15500  postgres
#   15600  nats (client)
#   15700  nats (monitoring)
#   15800  redis
#   15900  tempo   (traces)
#   15901  loki    (logs + the crash layer)
#   15902  mimir   (metrics)
#
# WHY NOT 5432, 4222, 6379. Those are the two or three most likely things
# already listening on a developer's machine: a Homebrew postgres, a redis from
# another project, a database GUI. `bin/dev` is the first command a new person
# runs on a repo they just cloned, and it is the worst possible moment to find
# out someone else owns 5432.
#
# A high range also makes two checkouts on one laptop a non-event, which matters
# because a developer working across two services has exactly that.
# tests/validate.sh asserts every published port is inside this block and that
# no two services reuse one, as a RANGE rather than a list — a list has to be
# edited every time a service is added, and a check that must be edited is one
# that gets skipped.
KIT_GRAFANA_PORT=15000
KIT_POSTGRES_PORT=15500
KIT_NATS_CLIENT_PORT=15600
KIT_NATS_MONITOR_PORT=15700
KIT_REDIS_PORT=15800
KIT_TEMPO_PORT=15900
KIT_LOKI_PORT=15901
KIT_MIMIR_PORT=15902

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- postgres
# Host port is set in the block above. Change it there rather than here.
KIT_POSTGRES_USER=cafaye
KIT_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=cafaye
# The platform database. A service creates its own database alongside it.
KIT_POSTGRES_DB=cafaye_platform
KIT_POSTGRES_TAG=16.6-alpine

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- nats
# The monitoring port serves /healthz and /jsz, and is what the healthcheck
# probes. It exposes subject names and stream state, so it stays on loopback.
KIT_NATS_TAG=2.10.24-alpine

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- redis
KIT_REDIS_TAG=7.4.1-alpine

# --------------------------------------------------------- otel collector
# Neither OTLP port is published to the host. These values are what services use
# to reach the collector over the compose network, by service name.
KIT_OTEL_GRPC_ENDPOINT=0.0.0.0:4317
KIT_OTEL_HTTP_ENDPOINT=0.0.0.0:4318
KIT_OTEL_HTTP_PORT=4318
KIT_OTEL_COLLECTOR_TAG=0.115.1
# The crash layer's intake, and NOT published to the host: it is where Docker's
# syslog log driver delivers every container's stderr, over the compose network
# only. A host port here would be an unauthenticated log-injection point for
# anything else on the machine.
KIT_OTEL_SYSLOG_ENDPOINT=0.0.0.0:15514
# memory_limiter: a percentage of the container's memory limit, not an absolute.
# Below 60 the collector starts shedding spans under a modest load spike, which
# looks like "my spans disappeared" to whoever is debugging.
KIT_OTEL_MEMORY_LIMIT_PERCENTAGE=75
KIT_OTEL_MEMORY_SPIKE_PERCENTAGE=15
# The collector's own ceiling, in the same spirit: six services plus four
# stores plus a collector is a lot of processes on one laptop, and an unbounded
# one is the process that gets the whole machine killed.
KIT_OTEL_MEMORY_LIMIT=384m
# batch: a 5s timeout is the usual local compromise — long enough that a
# request burst is one batch, short enough that `docker compose logs -f` shows
# the span while you are still looking at it.
KIT_OTEL_BATCH_TIMEOUT=5s
KIT_OTEL_BATCH_SIZE=512
# The redaction processor's diagnostic verbosity. `info` counts what it removed;
# `debug` also names the removed keys, which is the right setting when you are
# working out why a span is missing an attribute and the wrong one to ship,
# because a list of removed key NAMES is still a list you did not choose to
# publish. tests/canary_test.sh runs the collector with `debug` for exactly
# that reason.
KIT_OTEL_REDACTION_SUMMARY=info
# basic is the collector's own logging, not the debug exporter's output.
KIT_OTEL_LOG_LEVEL=info
KIT_OTEL_HEALTH_ENDPOINT=0.0.0.0:13133
# The debug exporter writes every span it receives. `json` is one span per line,
# which is what grep and `docker compose logs` can consume; `detailed` is for
# when you are reading one span by hand.
KIT_OTEL_DEBUG_VERBOSITY=normal
# Where the metrics go, and how often. The spanmetrics connector is what makes
# "one place to see all errors" a single query rather than six dashboards.
KIT_OTEL_METRIC_NAMESPACE=cafaye
KIT_OTEL_METRICS_FLUSH_INTERVAL=15s
# How long one export may take. A slow export is a latency bug in development
# caused by an observability tool, which is the worst possible source of one,
# so this is short rather than the OTel default of 30s.
KIT_OTEL_EXPORT_TIMEOUT=5s
# The three backends speak plaintext OTLP to each other, inside the compose
# network only. A self-hoster terminating TLS changes this and supplies the
# endpoint variables below; a literal endpoint never appears in any config.
KIT_OTEL_TLS_INSECURE=true
KIT_TEMPO_OTLP_ENDPOINT=tempo:4317
# Loki and Mimir take OTLP over HTTP, on their own ports. Neither has a gRPC
# listener: a gRPC exporter pointed at loki:4317 gets connection refused, and
# one pointed at mimir:9095 connects to the ingester RING and fails with
# `Unimplemented: unknown service ...MetricsService`. Both base URLs end in
# `/otlp` — the exporter appends `/v1/<signal>` itself.
KIT_LOKI_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://loki:3100/otlp
KIT_MIMIR_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://mimir:8080/otlp
# Mimir is multi-tenant by default and answers 401 to a write with no tenant.
# This is the one tenant a single-binary dev stack has; it matches the
# `overrides.defaults` key in tempo.yaml and the `single-tenant` in Grafana.
KIT_MIMIR_TENANT=single-tenant

# ============================================= the four backing services (LGTM)
#
# ALL FOUR ARE AGPL-3.0 AND ARE SHIPPED UNMODIFIED. The images below are stock
# `grafana/*` tags, mounted with read-only CONFIGURATION. Nothing is forked,
# patched or rebranded — that is the condition the licence cares about, and
# tests/validate.sh fails on a `build:` stanza on any of these four services.
#
# Pinned to an exact tag, never `latest`: `latest` for a log store means a
# self-hoster's upgrade path is whatever happened to be cached when their disk
# filled. The versions here are the ones the gate and the dashboards were built
# against; raise them in your own repo.
#
# The memory limits are per-service caps and deliberately conservative. A dev
# machine running six services plus four more needs every one of them bounded,
# or the kernel kills the largest and the whole thing gets blamed for whatever
# else was running.
KIT_TEMPO_TAG=2.7.2
KIT_LOKI_TAG=3.2.1
KIT_MIMIR_TAG=2.13.0
KIT_GRAFANA_TAG=11.3.0
KIT_TEMPO_MEMORY_LIMIT=256m
KIT_LOKI_MEMORY_LIMIT=256m
KIT_MIMIR_MEMORY_LIMIT=384m
KIT_GRAFANA_MEMORY_LIMIT=256m
# Grafana's dev credentials, for a port on your own machine and nothing else.
# The real ones live in each service's deployment secrets. If you find yourself
# wanting a production credential here, you want a secrets manager instead.
KIT_GRAFANA_USER=admin
KIT_GRAFANA_PASSWORD=admin

# ------------------------------------------------------------- service env
# A service's own .env holds these, not this file. They are listed here because
# bin/dev prints them and because a service that cannot find one fails with a
# much worse error message than "this variable is not set".
#
#   OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=<repo name, lowercase>          e.g. caf
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4318
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/protobuf
#   OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER=parentbased_traceidratio
#   OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER_ARG=1.0
#   OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES=deployment.environment=local,service.namespace=cafaye
#
# parentbased_traceidratio is not an accident: it defers to the caller's
# sampled flag, so a trace the edge service chose to record is not dropped
# halfway through by a service with a different opinion. See
# templates/otel/README.md.
